          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What 7,500 Running Watts Covers</h3>

          <p>
            Seventy-five hundred running watts is a lot of power for a house. It covers a refrigerator, a chest freezer, a gas furnace blower, a well pump, a window or small mini-split air conditioner, lights, a microwave used one at a time, and the router and chargers, all at once with room to spare. It will not run an electric range, an electric water heater and a central air system together, and nothing portable will.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the Peak Figure Is For</h3>

          <p>
            The peak figure is not useless. It tells you how much surge headroom you have when a motor starts while everything else is already running. A well pump that draws 1,000 watts running can pull three times that for a moment at start-up, and a 9,500 peak generator with 5,000 watts already loaded has about 4,500 watts of surge left for it. That headroom is the real reason to step up from a 6,500 or 8,000 watt unit.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Size on running watts, check surge on peak watts.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Add up the running watts of everything you want on at once and keep the total under about 80% of the running rating, around 6,000 watts here. Then check that your single biggest motor's starting surge fits under the peak rating with everything else running.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

