        <div id="thirty-or-fifty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. 30 Amps or 50 Amps: Which Transfer Switch Fits
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the decision that trips people up in this class, and it is worth settling before you choose a generator. A 30-amp, 240-volt connection through an L14-30 cord and inlet can carry 7,200 watts. A 7,500 to 7,600 running watt generator therefore fills a 30-amp connection completely and has a few hundred watts it cannot deliver through it. That is a very good match: you are not paying for capacity you cannot use, and 30-amp hardware is the cheapest and most common transfer switch setup there is.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">About the 50-Amp Outlet</h3>

          <p>
            Several inverters in this class, including the DuroMax and GENMAX listings, advertise a 50-amp outlet. A 50-amp receptacle on a 7,600 running watt generator does not give you 50 amps. At 240 volts, 7,600 watts is about 31.7 amps no matter which plug it comes out of. The 50-amp outlet only helps if you already own a 50-amp inlet and transfer switch, perhaps left over from a larger generator, or want to plug into an RV that takes a 14-50 cord. For a new install built around a generator of this size, a 30-amp inlet and switch is the right hardware.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Transfer Switch or Interlock</h3>

          <p>
            The two common ways to connect are a manual transfer switch with a set number of circuits, typically six to ten, or a breaker interlock kit on your main panel with a 30-amp back-feed breaker. The interlock lets you choose any circuit in the house, which suits this size well, since 7,200 watts is enough to be flexible about what you run. Either way the generator connects through an outdoor inlet box, never a double-ended cord into an outlet. Our guide to <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/">transfer switch types and installation costs</a> covers the hardware, and the <a href="/best-generator-inlet-box/">best generator inlet boxes</a> page covers the outside half of it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choosing the Cord</h3>

          <p>
            For the cord, a 30-amp L14-30 cord should be 10 gauge at the lengths most people need, 20 to 30 feet, and longer runs need heavier wire to avoid voltage drop. Our <a href="/generator-extension-cord-size/">generator extension cord sizing guide</a> has the table. Keep the cord length to what you need to put the generator at least 20 feet from the house, away from windows and doors.
          </p>
        </div>

