          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Central Air</h3>

          <p>
            Central air is the load people most often ask this class to carry. A 3-ton central air compressor runs at around 3,500 watts, which fits, but its starting surge can exceed the whole 9,500 peak rating on its own. With a soft starter installed on the condenser, start-up current drops sharply and a 7,500 running watt generator can usually start it, provided the rest of the load is light at that moment. Without one, expect the generator to bog down or trip. Our guide to <a href="/what-size-generator-runs-central-air-conditioning/">what size generator runs central air</a> walks through the math.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where the Class Runs Out</h3>

          <p>
            Electric water heaters, electric ranges and electric dryers are where the class runs out. A 4,500 watt water heater element alone takes most of the spare capacity, and running it alongside a well pump and refrigerator is asking for an overload. If those are on your must-run list, you need a 12,500 peak class generator or a standby unit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Base Load Plus One Big Load</h3>

          <p>
            The practical way to use a generator this size is to treat the base load of fridge, freezer, furnace, pump, lights and network as always on, and to schedule the big intermittent loads. Run the microwave, then the coffee maker, not both while the pump is filling. With that habit, 7,500 running watts feels close to normal life.
          </p>
