        <div id="inverter-or-open" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Inverter or Open Frame at This Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three of the five picks are inverters and two are conventional open-frame generators, and the choice between them is mostly about money, noise and what you plan to power. Open-frame units like the Westinghouse WGen7500c and Champion 201277 spin their engines at a fixed 3,600 rpm to hold 60 hertz, which makes them loud at every load. Inverters generate high-frequency power and convert it electronically, so their engines can slow down at light load, and they sit inside an enclosure that cuts noise further. The DuroMax is rated at about 61 dB at quarter load; open-frame units in this class are typically in the 70s.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Noise Difference</h3>

          <p>
            Ten decibels sounds small but is roughly a doubling of perceived loudness. On a quiet suburban street at night that is the difference between a neighbour hearing a hum and a neighbour knocking on the door. If your generator will sit close to the property line, or you expect week-long outages, the inverter premium buys real quality of life.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Power Quality</h3>

          <p>
            The power quality argument is narrower than marketing suggests. Most modern open-frame generators produce power that refrigerators, pumps and lights run on happily. The cases where inverter output clearly matters are high-efficiency gas furnaces whose control boards reject distorted power, some variable-speed equipment, and sensitive electronics you would rather not risk. If your furnace has refused to run on a borrowed generator in the past, buy an inverter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cost and Weight</h3>

          <p>
            The downside of inverters in this class is cost, often about twice an open-frame unit of the same running watts, and weight, since the enclosure adds bulk. Our explainer on <a href="/inverter-generator-vs-regular-generator/">inverter vs regular generators</a> goes deeper. For a house on a large lot that needs full 30-amp output for a few storms a year, the open-frame Westinghouse is the better use of money.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Fuel: Gasoline, Propane or Natural Gas
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fuel planning matters more at this size than below it, because a generator burning through 7,000 watts of load uses a lot of it. Westinghouse rates the WGen7500c at 11 hours at 25% load on a 6.6 gallon tank, which works out to about 0.6 gallons an hour at quarter load. At half load an open-frame engine of this size typically burns closer to a gallon an hour, so a realistic outage load of 4,000 to 5,000 watts can take 15 to 25 gallons a day. Storing that much treated gasoline safely is a real constraint.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Propane</h3>

          <p>
            Propane solves storage. It does not go stale, and a pair of 100 pound cylinders sits outside for years without attention. The cost is output, usually about a 10% drop: the Champion falls from 7,500 to 6,750 running watts on propane, while the Pulsar loses only 400 watts, from 7,600 to 7,200. Either is still close to what a 30-amp connection can carry. Propane is also bulkier per hour of run time, so a 20 pound grill tank lasts only a few hours at a moderate load. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-propane-does-a-generator-use/">how much propane a generator uses</a> has per-hour figures.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Natural Gas</h3>

          <p>
            Natural gas, on the tri fuel DuroMax XP9500iHT, removes fuel logistics entirely if your house has a gas service: a plumber installs a quick-connect near where the generator sits, and it runs as long as the utility pressure holds. Output on natural gas is lower than on propane, so check DuroMax's spec sheet and size your loads on that figure, not the gasoline one. For an owner who does not want to store fuel at all, it is the closest a portable gets to a standby generator. Our <a href="/best-tri-fuel-generator/">tri fuel generator guide</a> compares the other options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Monthly Exercise Runs</h3>

          <p>
            Whatever the fuel, run the generator for 15 to 20 minutes every month or two under some load. Carburetor varnish from old gasoline is the most common reason a generator will not start on the first night of an outage, and dual fuel owners can store the unit with the carburetor run dry and do their monthly exercise on propane.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What to Check Before You Buy
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Outlet Panel</h3>

          <p>
            Start with the panel. You want a 120/240-volt L14-30R twist-lock for the transfer switch, or a 14-50R if you are deliberately building around a 50-amp inlet, plus at least two GFCI-protected 120-volt duplex outlets for cords. Several listings in this class describe outlets loosely or differently from the manual, so zoom in on the panel photo and count the receptacles yourself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">CO Shutdown</h3>

          <p>
            Next, CO shutdown. Every pick here has a carbon monoxide sensor that shuts the engine off if CO builds up around it, and there is no reason to buy a home backup generator without one in 2026. It is not a substitute for placement: the generator still belongs at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away from doors and windows. Our guide on <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">where to place a generator outside</a> covers the details.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Starting Method</h3>

          <p>
            Starting method matters more than it seems. These engines are 420 to 460cc and a recoil start is a hard pull, especially in cold weather. Electric start is standard on everything here, and remote start on the Westinghouse, DuroMax and GENMAX means you can start the generator from a doorway in the rain. Keep the starter battery on a maintainer between outages, because a flat battery on the night you need it is a common complaint.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weight</h3>

          <p>
            Finally, weight. Every generator in this class weighs around 200 pounds or more. They all roll on wheels, but getting one into a truck bed or up a step needs two people or a ramp. If you will store it in a shed and roll it a short distance on a paved path, it does not matter. If you will move it over gravel or grass, check the wheel size and handle design in listing photos.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-other-sizes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Should You Buy a Size Up or Down Instead?
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Step Down</h3>

          <p>
            Step down to the <a href="/best-8000-watt-generator/">8,000 watt class</a> if your loads are a fridge, freezer, furnace and lights, with no well pump or air conditioner. Many 8,000 watt generators have 6,500 to 7,000 running watts, which handles that list easily for less money and less fuel. The 9,500 class earns its premium when a well pump and an air conditioner will both run, because that is where the extra surge headroom is used.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Step Up</h3>

          <p>
            Step up to the 10,000 to 12,500 peak class if you need 9,000 running watts or more: central air without a soft starter, an electric water heater, or two well pumps. Those generators pair with 50-amp inlets and switches, because they make more than a 30-amp connection can carry. They also burn more fuel at every load, so do not buy one for headroom you will rarely use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Adding Up Your Loads</h3>

          <p>
            If you are not sure of your loads, add them up with our <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/">generator wattage chart</a> or the walkthrough in <a href="/what-size-generator-do-i-need-for-my-house/">what size generator you need for your house</a>. Most households that do this land between 4,500 and 6,500 running watts, which is exactly the range a 9,500 peak generator handles with room to spare.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Standby Makes More Sense</h3>

          <p>
            One more option deserves a mention. If you want automatic start and never want to handle fuel or cords, a standby generator is a different purchase with different costs, starting at several thousand dollars installed. Our <a href="/best-portable-generator/">best portable generators</a> guide covers the full portable range and when a standby makes more sense.
          </p>
        </div>

