          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What will a 9500 watt generator run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With 7,500 to 7,600 running watts, a 9,500 peak generator runs a refrigerator, chest freezer, gas furnace blower, a 1 horsepower well pump, a window air conditioner, lights and electronics at the same time, with room for a microwave used in turns. It will not run an electric water heater, range and central air together.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 9500 watt generator enough for a house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For essentials, yes. Most homes need 4,500 to 6,500 running watts to keep the fridge, freezer, furnace, pump and lights on, which fits comfortably. It is not a whole-house generator for homes with electric heat, an electric water heater or central air without a soft starter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a 9500 watt generator run central air?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, if the condenser has a soft starter. A 3-ton unit runs at about 3,500 watts but can surge past 9,000 watts at start-up without one. With a soft starter fitted and the rest of the load kept light at that moment, a 7,500 running watt generator can usually start it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much fuel does a 9500 watt generator use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Westinghouse rates its WGen7500c at 11 hours at 25% load on 6.6 gallons, about 0.6 gallons an hour. At half load expect roughly a gallon an hour from an open-frame unit. Inverters use less at light loads because the engine slows down, and propane use is higher by volume.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a 30 amp or 50 amp transfer switch for a 9500 watt generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 30-amp switch is the right match. It carries 7,200 watts at 240 volts, and these generators make 7,500 to 7,600 running watts, so you lose very little. A 50-amp switch only makes sense if you already own one or plan to move to a larger generator.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is there a 9000 watt inverter generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. The GENMAX GM9000iED is sold as 9,000 watts and its manual lists 7,600 rated watts, the same running output as the 9,500 watt DuroMax and Pulsar inverters. The 500 watt difference is only in peak rating, which matters for a second or two when a large motor starts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Westinghouse WGen9500DF a 9500 watt generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in the sense this page uses. The WGen9500DF has 9,500 running watts and a 12,500 peak rating, so it belongs with the 12,000 watt class. The Westinghouse generators with a 9,500 peak rating are the WGen7500 models, including the WGen7500c and WGen7500DFc.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on the peak number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 9,500 peak generator makes 7,500 to 7,600 running watts. Plan your loads on that, and on the propane figure if you will run propane, rather than on the number printed in the largest type on the box. The gap between the two is the most common reason owners overload a new generator.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Wiring a 50-amp inlet for a 7,600 watt generator</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 50-amp receptacle does not make more power. At 240 volts, 7,600 watts is about 32 amps, so a 30-amp inlet, cord and switch carry nearly all of it for less money. Build around 50 amps only if a bigger generator is in your plans.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting central air without a soft starter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 3-ton condenser can surge past 9,000 watts at start-up, which is the entire peak rating. Install a soft starter on the condenser and start it while the rest of the load is light, or expect the generator to stall and trip.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Underestimating fuel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At a realistic outage load an open-frame unit can burn 15 to 25 gallons of gasoline a day. Work out how many days you want to cover, then decide whether you can store that much treated fuel or should run propane instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Back-feeding through a dryer outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A double-ended cord into a dryer outlet energises the utility line and can kill a lineworker. Use a transfer switch or a panel interlock with an inlet box, installed to code, and nothing else.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

